WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.010
Is it possible for a living thing to leave its own genetic text untouched and correct the messages coming out of it along the way?

2
00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:15.460
The octopus does exactly that: when it enters cold water, the protein recipes in its brain are rewritten at thousands of points within hours.

3
00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:19.430
But this ability carries a heavy price no one expected.

4
00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.880
Picture an octopus tucked beneath a weed-covered ledge of rock off the coast of California.

5
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:42.770
The moment it registers an approaching shadow, its skin takes on the colour of the rock in a fraction of a second, and the texture is imitated as well; a smooth surface roughens.

6
00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:45.620
That is the octopus's famous trick.

7
00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:49.270
But the animal's real strangeness is not in its skin.

8
00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:53.720
It happens somewhere no one can see, inside its nerve cells.

9
00:00:53.720 --> 00:01:03.290
There, even while the skin is changing colour, a process runs on quietly: the creature is correcting not its genetic instruction, but the copy of that instruction.

10
00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:08.380
To understand what that means, we have to recall the basic flow inside a cell.

11
00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.590
DNA is an archive.

12
00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:15.520
Locked, untouchable, handed down from generation to generation.

13
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:23.010
If a protein is to be made, the cell does not take the relevant page out of that archive; it makes a copy of the page.

14
00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:26.020
This copy is called messenger RNA.

15
00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:35.750
The copy travels to a production bench called the ribosome, the bench reads the letters on the copy in groups of three, and for every triplet it lays down one amino acid.

16
00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:39.400
A protein comes out the other end.

17
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.690
The entire logic of the system rests on the copy being faithful.

18
00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:47.500
Whatever is written in the archive is what must reach the bench.

19
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:53.390
In the octopus, that fidelity breaks down, and not by accident but systematically.

20
00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:57.520
Before the copy reaches the bench, an enzyme steps in.

21
00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.050
Its name is ADAR.

22
00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:06.740
What it does is chemically almost laughably small: it snaps an amino group off an adenosine letter in the RNA.

23
00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.750
What remains is a molecule called inosine.

24
00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:15.640
But the ribosome does not recognise inosine; when it looks at it, it sees guanosine.

25
00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:21.850
So for the cell's reading apparatus, that letter is no longer an A, it is a G.

26
00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:24.860
Nothing in the archive has changed.

27
00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:29.310
The only thing that changed is the copy made for this one occasion.

28
00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:39.200
A single letter usually means nothing; thanks to the redundancy of the genetic code, the same amino acid gets laid down even when the last letter of a triplet changes.

29
00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.890
But sometimes it does mean something.

30
00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:53.540
The triplet AUA, for instance, means isoleucine; if the adenosine in the middle is edited, the triplet becomes AUG and the bench puts methionine there instead of isoleucine.

31
00:02:53.540 --> 00:03:01.990
That single-letter intervention can change how the protein folds, how fast it passes an ion through, how tightly it grips another protein.

32
00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:09.000
The same gene, in the same cell, at the same moment, begins to produce proteins that differ from one another.

33
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.490
This mechanism is not unique to the octopus.

34
00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:16.620
Humans have ADAR enzymes too, and they are hardly idle.

35
00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:28.910
But in humans the overwhelming majority of edits take place in the non-protein-coding repeat regions of the genome; those that change a letter of a protein number a few thousand, and the ones conserved across mammals,

36
00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:34.000
meaning the ones evolution is evidently watching over, are only a few dozen.

37
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.410
The system exists in us, but it essentially stands off to the side.

38
00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:42.580
In cephalopods it has moved to the centre.

39
00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:50.710
Between 80 and 130 thousand editing sites have been identified in the protein-coding regions of the octopus, the squid and the cuttlefish.

40
00:03:50.710 --> 00:04:02.520
In a single species, the California two-spot octopus, known scientifically as Octopus bimaculoides, RNA taken from 12 different tissues revealed more than 900 thousand editing sites;

41
00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:07.930
roughly 12 percent of them lay in regions that directly alter the letters of a protein.

42
00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:12.860
Of the edits in coding regions, 65 percent change an amino acid.

43
00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:18.910
This is not an occasional accident; it is part of the routine operation of the nervous system.

44
00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:22.880
Comparison is where the strangeness of all this really shows.

45
00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:33.730
In the nautilus, which comes from the oldest branch of the cephalopods and has kept its shell for hundreds of millions of years, and in a relative among the molluscs, the sea hare Aplysia,

46
00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:37.540
the same analysis yields about 1000 sites.

47
00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:41.990
That number is indistinguishable from the noise level of the method itself.

48
00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:47.400
So the habit of heavily rewriting one's RNA is not a shared inheritance of all molluscs.

49
00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:55.850
It appeared after the branch leading to the nautilus split off, in the ancestor of the soft-bodied cephalopods, and it stayed there.

50
00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:59.980
What is more, these edits are not scattered at random.

51
00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:10.510
There are 1146 editing sites conserved across the squid, the cuttlefish and two octopus species all at once, and they cluster on 443 different proteins.

52
00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:18.640
For a feature to sit in the same place, on the same letter, in four separate lineages means evolution is keeping watch over those points.

53
00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:25.970
And when you look at what those 443 proteins are, the picture sharpens: predominantly molecules that work in the nervous system.

54
00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:32.020
Ion channels, synapse proteins, motor proteins that haul cargo inside the cell.

55
00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:37.430
In other words, the octopus edits its RNA in the very place where it does its thinking.

56
00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:39.800
So why?

57
00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:46.810
When an organism could write protein diversity straight into its gene, why would it work through the copy every single time?

58
00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:51.260
The first serious answer came from the poles, in 2011.

59
00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:59.390
Researchers compared the potassium channel genes that do the same job in an octopus living in Antarctic waters and one living in tropical waters.

60
00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:08.160
The two genes differed at only 4 positions of the protein they encode, and when produced separately in the laboratory the channels behaved almost identically.

61
00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:13.250
The genes did not account for the gulf between the worlds the two animals inhabit.

62
00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:16.580
What created the difference was in the RNA.

63
00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:25.990
The channel's messenger RNA was being heavily edited, and one site was especially striking: an edit that turns an isoleucine in the channel's pore into a valine.

64
00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:32.040
This single change destabilised the channel's open state and markedly increased its closing speed.

65
00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:40.010
And that site was extensively edited in Antarctic and Arctic species, while in tropical species it was largely left unedited.

66
00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:45.260
The intensity of the editing tracked the temperature of the water the animal had been caught in.

67
00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:51.630
Cold water slows the movement of molecules; a nerve cell is delayed in pulling its signal back together.

68
00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:59.760
The polar octopus was compensating for that delay not by changing its gene, but by changing a single letter of the copy coming off that gene.

69
00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:03.090
That was an observation across species.

70
00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:08.340
The real question was this: can a single animal do it within its own lifetime?

71
00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:12.470
An experiment published in 2023 asked exactly that.

72
00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:23.480
In work led by Joshua Rosenthal of the Marine Biological Laboratory and Eli Eisenberg of Tel Aviv University, California octopuses were placed in temperature-controlled aquariums.

73
00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:29.050
One group at 22 degrees, one group at 13 degrees, for 2 to 3 weeks.

74
00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:32.700
Then all the RNA in their nervous tissue was read.

75
00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:39.870
In the animals kept in the cold, editing levels had shifted at more than 13 thousand sites that change a letter of a protein.

76
00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:46.400
At roughly a third of the protein-altering sites, editing was higher at 13 degrees than at 22.

77
00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:55.330
And this was not happening on a seasonal timescale: it began within hours of the temperature change and settled into its new equilibrium in about 4 days.

78
00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:59.780
The researchers followed two proteins all the way down to their function.

79
00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:05.830
One was kinesin-1, a motor that hauls cargo along the cell's long extensions.

80
00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:13.320
The other was synaptotagmin, the molecule that senses calcium at the synapse and triggers the emptying of a vesicle.

81
00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:18.890
The cold-edited kinesin showed a different profile of movement along microtubules.

82
00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:23.500
The edited synaptotagmin's calcium-binding behaviour changed.

83
00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:31.470
So the octopus is not merely swapping letters; it is retuning the transport and communication hardware of its nervous system for the cold.

84
00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:35.280
Why that matters is explained by the animal's predicament.

85
00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:40.210
An octopus cannot regulate its body temperature; it is whatever the water is.

86
00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:45.300
Off the coast of southern California the seasonal swing is quite wide.

87
00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:49.750
Changing a gene takes generations and runs in one direction only.

88
00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:52.920
But editing a copy can be undone.

89
00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:58.810
When the water warms, the editing rate comes back down and the animal returns to its former setting.

90
00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:02.620
The mechanism itself is still not fully solved.

91
00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:11.550
The strongest explanation is this: for ADAR to work, the target RNA has to fold back on itself and form a double-stranded structure.

92
00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:14.240
Cold makes such folds more stable.

93
00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:21.890
So no separate thermostat may be needed to make the enzyme work harder; the physics of RNA is already temperature-sensitive.

94
00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:31.940
That is a plausible explanation, but whether it accounts for everything on its own, and whether the enzyme itself is separately regulated as well, remains an open research question;

95
00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:35.110
it should not be told as a settled result.

96
00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:38.120
There is a spatial dimension to this too.

97
00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:43.690
A study on the giant axon of the squid showed that editing does not happen only in the nucleus.

98
00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:50.860
The ADAR enzyme is also found outside the cell nucleus, inside the axon, and it does its work there as well.

99
00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:55.630
Thousands of sites are more heavily edited in the axon than in the cell body.

100
00:09:55.630 --> 00:10:02.160
This means the cell can give separate answers not only to the question of when, but also to the question of where.

101
00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:05.810
Everything described so far looks like a free ability.

102
00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:07.540
It is not.

103
00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:10.870
ADAR does not pick an adenosine at random.

104
00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:19.640
For the enzyme to find its target, the messenger RNA has to fold in a particular way, and that adenosine has to sit inside a double-stranded structure.

105
00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:31.770
That structure, in turn, is built when another piece of RNA comes along and pairs with it; sometimes this is a nearby sequence, sometimes a distant region that will never be translated into protein at all.

106
00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:40.220
The consequence is this: the sequences surrounding the editing site and pairing with it have to stay put just as much as the site itself does.

107
00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:43.230
For a genome, that is a heavy constraint.

108
00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:52.320
Normally most mutations in a sequence are harmless; they do not change a letter of the protein, evolution does not interfere with them, and they accumulate.

109
00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:58.690
But in a creature that does editing, that same mutation can wreck the folding without damaging the protein at all.

110
00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:06.020
If the folding is wrecked, ADAR cannot find the site, the editing stops, and the animal loses one of its settings.

111
00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:12.230
So natural selection weeds out, here, mutations it does not care about in other species.

112
00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:16.360
A study published in 2017 measured that cost.

113
00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:26.410
In the protein-coding sequences of soft-bodied cephalopods, the number of single-letter differences accumulating around editing sites came out between 10 and 26 percent lower.

114
00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:32.140
Between 3 and 15 percent of the mutations expected between species had been cleared away.

115
00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:39.310
In other words, these animals have paid for the flexibility they gained on the copy with the rate of change they lost on the archive.

116
00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:46.480
The idea of a trade-off in evolution is nothing new; usually we talk about energy, growth, reproductive rate.

117
00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:51.730
The trade-off here involves something stranger: the capacity for change itself.

118
00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:58.900
In order to adapt to cold within hours today, the octopus has given up a portion of its genome's freedom to move in the future.

119
00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:05.590
The nautilus never entered that bargain; it stayed with its shell, its slowness, its unedited RNA.

120
00:12:05.590 --> 00:12:09.880
Both strategies have been standing for hundreds of millions of years.

121
00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:13.530
The octopus genome itself completes this picture.

122
00:12:13.530 --> 00:12:22.780
The genome sequence published in 2015 offered no explanation of the expected kind; there had been no shortcut event such as a whole-genome duplication.

123
00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:32.190
Instead there was a large expansion in the protocadherin family, which handles recognition at the cell surface; the octopus has nearly twice as many of these as humans do.

124
00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:37.280
But the diversity of the proteome goes far beyond any scale that gene count could explain.

125
00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:44.610
In this creature, a substantial part of the diversity is produced not at the level of the gene, but at the level of the copy.

126
00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:53.060
And looked at from today, the most striking part of the story is this: humans are deliberately trying to do what the octopus does as a matter of routine.

127
00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:55.430
The idea is simple.

128
00:12:55.430 --> 00:12:59.080
The ADAR enzyme is already in the cell.

129
00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:06.890
It may be possible to steer it to a chosen adenosine with a short guide molecule, without introducing any enzyme from outside.

130
00:13:06.890 --> 00:13:14.220
In October of 2024, the first therapeutic RNA editing in a human being was reported using this approach.

131
00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:26.350
The target was an inherited disease called alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency; in the most common form of the disease, a single-letter mutation turns a glutamic acid at one position of the protein into a lysine.

132
00:13:26.350 --> 00:13:36.560
An A-to-I edit is precisely the kind of intervention that can turn that letter back, and the first results measured in the skin showed that the corrected protein really did appear in the blood.

133
00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:42.290
This approach differs from DNA editing in one clear way: the archive is not touched.

134
00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:44.660
What is done is temporary.

135
00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:49.270
If the drug is stopped the effect fades, leaving no permanent trace.

136
00:13:49.270 --> 00:13:55.640
That is an advantage in terms of safety and a burden in terms of treatment; it requires repeating for life.

137
00:13:55.640 --> 00:14:02.330
And it is still at a very early stage; few patients, short follow-up, long-term outcomes unknown.

138
00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:06.140
It is too early to draw any definitive victory from it.

139
00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:10.590
What the octopus really leaves behind is not a treatment but a way of seeing.

140
00:14:10.590 --> 00:14:19.040
For a long time we read the genome as the creature's complete and final instruction; if the flaw is there, we said, the solution is there too.

141
00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:26.690
That animal waiting out the cooling water in a hollow of rock shows that there is an editorial desk between the archive and the action.

142
00:14:26.690 --> 00:14:31.620
The enzyme sitting at that desk can change what is said without changing what is written.

143
00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:37.830
But the desk has a price of its own: the paper beneath it has to stay still for the correction to remain possible.

144
00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:41.480
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

145
00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:45.130
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
